Let $f(x)$ be the polynomial function defined by the left-hand side of the given equation:
\[
f(x) = x^3 - 15x + 1.
\]
We seek to show that there exist three distinct values $c \in [-4, 4]$ such that $f(c) = 0$.

Since $f(x)$ is a polynomial, it is continuous and differentiable everywhere on $\mathbb{R}$. Therefore, we may apply the **Intermediate Value Theorem (IVT)**. The IVT states that if a function $f$ is continuous on a closed interval $[a, b]$ and $k$ is any number between $f(a)$ and $f(b)$, then there exists at least one number $c \in (a, b)$ such that $f(c) = k$. Specifically, if $f(a)$ and $f(b)$ have opposite signs, there exists a root in $(a, b)$.

To locate the roots, we evaluate $f(x)$ at integer values within the interval $[-4, 4]$ to identify sign changes.

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Evaluation at $x = -4$ and $x = -3$:}
    \begin{align*}
        f(-4) &= (-4)^3 - 15(-4) + 1 = -64 + 60 + 1 = -3, \\
        f(-3) &= (-3)^3 - 15(-3) + 1 = -27 + 45 + 1 = 19.
    \end{align*}
    Since $f(-4) < 0$ and $f(-3) > 0$, by the Intermediate Value Theorem, there exists a solution $c_1 \in (-4, -3)$.

    \item \textbf{Evaluation at $x = 0$ and $x = 1$:}
    \begin{align*}
        f(0) &= (0)^3 - 15(0) + 1 = 1, \\
        f(1) &= (1)^3 - 15(1) + 1 = 1 - 15 + 1 = -13.
    \end{align*}
    Since $f(0) > 0$ and $f(1) < 0$, by the Intermediate Value Theorem, there exists a solution $c_2 \in (0, 1)$.

    \item \textbf{Evaluation at $x = 3$ and $x = 4$:}
    \begin{align*}
        f(3) &= (3)^3 - 15(3) + 1 = 27 - 45 + 1 = -17, \\
        f(4) &= (4)^3 - 15(4) + 1 = 64 - 60 + 1 = 5.
    \end{align*}
    Since $f(3) < 0$ and $f(4) > 0$, by the Intermediate Value Theorem, there exists a solution $c_3 \in (3, 4)$.
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Conclusion:}
We have identified three intervals containing roots:
\[
c_1 \in (-4, -3), \quad c_2 \in (0, 1), \quad \text{and} \quad c_3 \in (3, 4).
\]
Since these intervals are disjoint (they do not overlap), the roots $c_1, c_2,$ and $c_3$ are distinct. Furthermore, all three intervals are subsets of $[-4, 4]$.

As $f(x)$ is a polynomial of degree 3, it can have at most three real roots. We have found exactly three distinct real roots within the specified interval.

Thus, the equation $x^3 - 15x + 1 = 0$ has three solutions in the interval $[-4, 4]$. \qed

