Solution:
\textbf{Definition of Riemann Integrability}

To determine whether a bounded function $f$ is integrable on $[a,b]$, we use the definition of the Riemann integral via Darboux sums. Let $P = \{x_0, x_1, \dots, x_n\}$ be a partition of $[a,b]$. The upper and lower Darboux sums are defined as:
\begin{align*}
U(f,P) &= \sum_{i=1}^n M_i \Delta x_i, \quad \text{where } M_i = \sup_{x \in [x_{i-1}, x_i]} f(x) \\
L(f,P) &= \sum_{i=1}^n m_i \Delta x_i, \quad \text{where } m_i = \inf_{x \in [x_{i-1}, x_i]} f(x)
\end{align*}
A function $f$ is Riemann integrable on $[a,b]$ if and only if for every $\epsilon > 0$, there exists a partition $P$ such that $U(f,P) - L(f,P) < \epsilon$. We will apply this criterion to each of the given functions.

\vspace{1em}

\begin{enumerate}

    \item \textbf{Function 1:} 
    \begin{align*}
    f(x) =
    \begin{cases}
    1 & \text{if } x < 1 \\
    2 & \text{if } x = 1
    \end{cases}
    \end{align*}

    \textbf{Conclusion:} Integrable.

    \textbf{Proof:} Let $\epsilon > 0$. We want to construct a partition $P$ such that $U(f,P) - L(f,P) < \epsilon$. Let $\delta = \min\left(\frac{1}{2}, \frac{\epsilon}{2}\right)$. Choose the partition $P = \{0, 1 - \delta, 1\}$. 

    This partition divides $[0,1]$ into two subintervals: $I_1 = [0, 1 - \delta]$ and $I_2 = [1 - \delta, 1]$.
    \begin{itemize}
        \item On $I_1$, $f(x) = 1$ for all $x$. Thus, $M_1 = 1$ and $m_1 = 1$, which gives $M_1 - m_1 = 0$.
        \item On $I_2$, $f(x)$ takes the value $1$ on $[1-\delta, 1)$ and $2$ at $x=1$. Thus, $M_2 = 2$ and $m_2 = 1$, which gives $M_2 - m_2 = 1$.
    \end{itemize}
    Now, we calculate the difference between the upper and lower sums:
    \begin{align*}
    U(f,P) - L(f,P) &= (M_1 - m_1)\Delta x_1 + (M_2 - m_2)\Delta x_2 \\
    &= 0 \cdot (1 - \delta) + 1 \cdot \delta \\
    &= \delta \le \frac{\epsilon}{2} < \epsilon
    \end{align*}
    Since we found such a partition for an arbitrary $\epsilon > 0$, $f$ is Riemann integrable.

    \vspace{1em}

    \item \textbf{Function 2:}
    \begin{align*}
    f(x) =
    \begin{cases}
    1 + x & \text{if } x \in \mathbb{Q} \\
    1 - x & \text{if } x \notin \mathbb{Q}
    \end{cases}
    \end{align*}

    \textbf{Conclusion:} Not integrable.

    \textbf{Proof:} Let $P = \{x_0, x_1, \dots, x_n\}$ be any partition of $[0,1]$. Consider an arbitrary subinterval $I_i = [x_{i-1}, x_i]$. Because both the rational numbers $\mathbb{Q}$ and the irrational numbers $\mathbb{R} \setminus \mathbb{Q}$ are dense in $\mathbb{R}$, every subinterval $I_i$ contains both rational and irrational numbers.

    Notice that for all $x \in [0,1]$, $1+x \ge 1-x$. Therefore, the supremum of $f$ on $I_i$ is determined by the rational values, and the infimum is determined by the irrational values:
    \begin{align*}
    M_i &= \sup_{x \in I_i} f(x) = \sup_{x \in I_i} (1+x) = 1 + x_i \\
    m_i &= \inf_{x \in I_i} f(x) = \inf_{x \in I_i} (1-x) = 1 - x_i
    \end{align*}
    The difference between the supremum and infimum on $I_i$ is:
    \begin{align*}
    M_i - m_i = (1 + x_i) - (1 - x_i) = 2x_i
    \end{align*}
    Now, we evaluate the difference between the Darboux sums:
    \begin{align*}
    U(f,P) - L(f,P) &= \sum_{i=1}^n (M_i - m_i) \Delta x_i = \sum_{i=1}^n 2x_i \Delta x_i
    \end{align*}
    Observe that $\sum_{i=1}^n 2x_i \Delta x_i$ is exactly the upper Darboux sum $U(g,P)$ for the continuous function $g(x) = 2x$ on $[0,1]$. Since $g(x)$ is integrable, its upper Darboux sum is bounded below by its integral:
    \begin{align*}
    U(f,P) - L(f,P) = U(g,P) \ge \int_0^1 2x \, dx = 1
    \end{align*}
    Because $U(f,P) - L(f,P) \ge 1$ for \textit{every} partition $P$, the difference cannot be made arbitrarily small (for instance, it can never be less than $\epsilon = 1/2$). Thus, $f$ is not Riemann integrable.

    \vspace{1em}

    \item \textbf{Function 3:}
    \begin{align*}
    f(x) =
    \begin{cases}
    \sin x & \text{if } x = \frac{1}{n},\ n \in \mathbb{N} \\
    \cos x & \text{otherwise}
    \end{cases}
    \end{align*}

    \textbf{Conclusion:} Integrable.

    \textbf{Proof:} First, note that $f(x)$ is bounded on $[0,1]$ because $|f(x)| \le 1$ for all $x$. Thus, for any subinterval, $M_i - m_i \le 2$. The function is continuous everywhere except at $x=0$ and at $x = \frac{1}{n}$ for $n \in \mathbb{N}$. 

    Let $\epsilon > 0$. We will construct a partition $P$ in three steps to isolate the discontinuities:

    \textit{Step 1: Isolate the infinite cluster of discontinuities near $0$.} 
    Choose an integer $N$ large enough so that $\frac{2}{N} < \frac{\epsilon}{3}$. We make $x_1 = \frac{1}{N}$ the first point in our partition. The contribution of the interval $[0, \frac{1}{N}]$ to the Darboux sum difference is at most:
    \begin{align*}
    (M_1 - m_1) \Delta x_1 \le 2 \cdot \frac{1}{N} < \frac{\epsilon}{3}
    \end{align*}

    \textit{Step 2: Isolate the remaining finitely many discontinuities.}
    On the remaining interval $[\frac{1}{N}, 1]$, $f(x)$ has exactly $N-1$ discontinuities, located at $d_k = \frac{1}{k}$ for $k = 1, 2, \dots, N-1$. We can enclose each $d_k$ in an open subinterval such that the sum of the lengths of these $N-1$ subintervals is strictly less than $\frac{\epsilon}{6}$. The contribution of these subintervals to $U(f,P) - L(f,P)$ is at most:
    \begin{align*}
    2 \cdot \left(\text{total length} \right) < 2 \cdot \frac{\epsilon}{6} = \frac{\epsilon}{3}
    \end{align*}

    \textit{Step 3: Bound the continuous portions.}
    Removing these open subintervals from $[\frac{1}{N}, 1]$ leaves a finite union of closed intervals. On these closed intervals, $f(x) = \cos x$, which is uniformly continuous. Therefore, we can partition these closed intervals finely enough such that $M_i - m_i < \frac{\epsilon}{3}$ on each subinterval. Since the total length of these continuous portions is less than $1$, their contribution to $U(f,P) - L(f,P)$ is strictly less than:
    \begin{align*}
    \frac{\epsilon}{3} \cdot 1 = \frac{\epsilon}{3}
    \end{align*}

    Combining the partition points from all three steps yields a partition $P$ of $[0,1]$ for which:
    \begin{align*}
    U(f,P) - L(f,P) < \frac{\epsilon}{3} + \frac{\epsilon}{3} + \frac{\epsilon}{3} = \epsilon
    \end{align*}
    Therefore, $f$ is Riemann integrable.

    \vspace{1em}

    \item \textbf{Function 4:}
    \begin{align*}
    f(x) =
    \begin{cases}
    \cos\left(\frac{\pi}{x}\right) & \text{if } x \in \mathbb{Q} \\
    0 & \text{if } x \notin \mathbb{Q}
    \end{cases}
    \end{align*}

    \textbf{Conclusion:} Not integrable.

    \textbf{Proof:} Let $P = \{x_0, x_1, \dots, x_n\}$ be any partition of $[0,1]$. Consider an arbitrary subinterval $I_i = [x_{i-1}, x_i]$. Due to the density of irrational numbers, there are points in $I_i$ where $f(x) = 0$. Thus, the infimum $m_i \le 0$ and the supremum $M_i \ge 0$.

    Due to the density of rational numbers, the supremum and infimum of $f(x)$ on $I_i$ are bounded by the extrema of $\cos\left(\frac{\pi}{x}\right)$. Specifically:
    \begin{align*}
    M_i &= \max\left(0, \sup_{x \in I_i} \cos\left(\frac{\pi}{x}\right)\right) \\
    m_i &= \min\left(0, \inf_{x \in I_i} \cos\left(\frac{\pi}{x}\right)\right)
    \end{align*}
    We claim that for any subinterval $I_i$, the difference $M_i - m_i \ge \sup_{x \in I_i} \left| \cos\left(\frac{\pi}{x}\right) \right|$. We prove this by considering three cases for the behavior of $\cos\left(\frac{\pi}{x}\right)$ on $I_i$:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item \textit{Case 1:} $\cos\left(\frac{\pi}{x}\right) \ge 0$ for all $x \in I_i$. Then $m_i = 0$ and $M_i = \sup_{I_i} \cos\left(\frac{\pi}{x}\right) = \sup_{I_i} \left| \cos\left(\frac{\pi}{x}\right) \right|$. Thus, $M_i - m_i = \sup_{I_i} \left| \cos\left(\frac{\pi}{x}\right) \right|$.
        \item \textit{Case 2:} $\cos\left(\frac{\pi}{x}\right) \le 0$ for all $x \in I_i$. Then $M_i = 0$ and $m_i = \inf_{I_i} \cos\left(\frac{\pi}{x}\right)$. Thus, $M_i - m_i = -\inf_{I_i} \cos\left(\frac{\pi}{x}\right) = \sup_{I_i} \left| \cos\left(\frac{\pi}{x}\right) \right|$.
        \item \textit{Case 3:} $\cos\left(\frac{\pi}{x}\right)$ takes both positive and negative values on $I_i$. Then $M_i = \sup_{I_i} \cos\left(\frac{\pi}{x}\right) > 0$ and $m_i = \inf_{I_i} \cos\left(\frac{\pi}{x}\right) < 0$. The difference is $M_i - m_i = \sup_{I_i} \cos\left(\frac{\pi}{x}\right) + \left| \inf_{I_i} \cos\left(\frac{\pi}{x}\right) \right|$. Since both terms are positive, their sum is strictly greater than either individual term, meaning $M_i - m_i \ge \max\left(\sup_{I_i} \cos\left(\frac{\pi}{x}\right), \left| \inf_{I_i} \cos\left(\frac{\pi}{x}\right) \right| \right) = \sup_{I_i} \left| \cos\left(\frac{\pi}{x}\right) \right|$.
    \end{itemize}
    In all cases, $M_i - m_i \ge \sup_{x \in I_i} \left| \cos\left(\frac{\pi}{x}\right) \right|$. 

    Now, we evaluate the difference between the Darboux sums:
    \begin{align*}
    U(f,P) - L(f,P) &= \sum_{i=1}^n (M_i - m_i) \Delta x_i \ge \sum_{i=1}^n \left(\sup_{x \in I_i} \left| \cos\left(\frac{\pi}{x}\right) \right| \right) \Delta x_i
    \end{align*}
    The rightmost sum is exactly the upper Darboux sum $U(g,P)$ for the function $g(x) = \left| \cos\left(\frac{\pi}{x}\right) \right|$. Since $g(x)$ is continuous on $(0,1]$ and bounded, it is Riemann integrable. Therefore, its upper Darboux sum is bounded below by its integral:
    \begin{align*}
    U(f,P) - L(f,P) \ge U(g,P) \ge \int_0^1 \left| \cos\left(\frac{\pi}{x}\right) \right| \, dx = I
    \end{align*}
    Because $g(x)$ is non-negative, continuous almost everywhere, and not identically zero, the integral $I$ is strictly positive ($I > 0$). Since $U(f,P) - L(f,P) \ge I > 0$ for \textit{every} partition $P$, the difference cannot be made arbitrarily small. Thus, $f$ is not Riemann integrable.

    \vspace{1em}

    \item \textbf{Function 5:}
    \begin{align*}
    f(x) =
    \begin{cases}
    x \left[ \frac{1}{x} \right] & \text{if } 0 < x \leq 1 \\
    0 & \text{if } x = 0
    \end{cases}
    \end{align*}

    \textbf{Conclusion:} Integrable.

    \textbf{Proof:} First, we establish that $f(x)$ is bounded. By the definition of the greatest integer function, for any $y \in \mathbb{R}$, $y - 1 < [y] \le y$. Substituting $y = \frac{1}{x}$ for $x > 0$, we get:
    \begin{align*}
    \frac{1}{x} - 1 < \left[ \frac{1}{x} \right] \le \frac{1}{x}
    \end{align*}
    Multiplying the entire inequality by $x > 0$ yields:
    \begin{align*}
    1 - x < x \left[ \frac{1}{x} \right] \le 1
    \end{align*}
    Thus, $0 \le f(x) \le 1$ for all $x \in [0,1]$. This means that for any subinterval, $M_i - m_i \le 1$.

    The function $f(x)$ is continuous everywhere except at $x=0$ and at points where $\frac{1}{x}$ is an integer (i.e., $x = \frac{1}{n}$ for $n \in \mathbb{N}$). This is the exact same discontinuity structure as Function 3, and we can apply the identical logic.

    Let $\epsilon > 0$. We construct a partition $P$ in three steps:

    \textit{Step 1: Isolate $0$.} 
    Choose an integer $N$ such that $\frac{1}{N} < \frac{\epsilon}{3}$. The interval $[0, \frac{1}{N}]$ contributes at most $1 \cdot \frac{1}{N} < \frac{\epsilon}{3}$ to $U(f,P) - L(f,P)$.

    \textit{Step 2: Isolate the remaining finitely many discontinuities.}
    On $[\frac{1}{N}, 1]$, the function has $N-1$ discontinuities at $x = \frac{1}{k}$ for $k = 1, \dots, N-1$. We enclose these in open subintervals of total length strictly less than $\frac{\epsilon}{3}$. Their contribution to the Darboux sum difference is at most $1 \cdot \frac{\epsilon}{3} = \frac{\epsilon}{3}$.

    \textit{Step 3: Bound the continuous portions.}
    The remaining set in $[\frac{1}{N}, 1]$ is a finite union of closed intervals. On each of these intervals, $\left[ \frac{1}{x} \right]$ is a constant integer $k$, so $f(x) = kx$. Since linear functions are uniformly continuous, we can partition these closed intervals finely enough such that $M_i - m_i < \frac{\epsilon}{3}$ on each subinterval. The total length of these portions is less than $1$, so their contribution is strictly less than $\frac{\epsilon}{3} \cdot 1 = \frac{\epsilon}{3}$.

    Summing the contributions from all parts of the partition $P$, we get:
    \begin{align*}
    U(f,P) - L(f,P) < \frac{\epsilon}{3} + \frac{\epsilon}{3} + \frac{\epsilon}{3} = \epsilon
    \end{align*}
    Therefore, $f$ is Riemann integrable.

\end{enumerate}

