    (A - 3I)\mathbf{v}_1 = \begin{pmatrix} -2 & 4 \\ 1 & -2 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} v_{11} \\ v_{12} \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix} \implies \mathbf{v}_1 = \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}
\end{align*}
For $\lambda_2 = -1$:
\begin{align*}
    (A + I)\mathbf{v}_2 = \begin{pmatrix} 2 & 4 \\ 1 & 2 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} v_{21} \\ v_{22} \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix} \implies \mathbf{v}_2 = \begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}
\end{align*}
The complementary solution is:
\begin{align*}
    \mathbf{x}_c(t) = c_1 e^{3t} \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} + c_2 e^{-t} \begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}
\end{align*}

To find the particular solution, we again use the method of undetermined coefficients. We assume $\mathbf{x}_p(t) = \mathbf{a} \cos t + \mathbf{b} \sin t$. Substituting into the non-homogeneous system and equating coefficients of $\cos t$ and $\sin t$, we obtain the same algebraic structure as in Problem 2:
\begin{align*}
    \mathbf{b} &= A\mathbf{a} + \mathbf{u} \\
    -\mathbf{a} &= A\mathbf{b} + \mathbf{v}
\end{align*}
where $\mathbf{u} = \begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ -1 \end{pmatrix}$ and $\mathbf{v} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}$. Substituting $\mathbf{b}$ into the second equation gives $(I + A^2)\mathbf{a} = -A\mathbf{u} - \mathbf{v}$. We compute $A^2$:
\begin{align*}
    A^2 = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 4 \\ 1 & 1 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 4 \\ 1 & 1 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 5 & 8 \\ 2 & 5 \end{pmatrix}
\end{align*}
Thus, $I + A^2 = \begin{pmatrix} 6 & 8 \\ 2 & 6 \end{pmatrix}$. Next, we compute the right-hand side vector $-A\mathbf{u} - \mathbf{v}$:
\begin{align*}
    A\mathbf{u} &= \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 4 \\ 1 & 1 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ -1 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} -6 \\ -3 \end{pmatrix} \\
